HEADLINE: NEW DEVICE WILL MAKE CALLS PRIVATE

BYLINE: FROM WIRE REPORTS

DATELINE: WASHINGTON

BODY:

A new device developed by the government will create new privacy for
cellular phone calls and other transmissions but still allow legal
authorities to listen in, the White House said yesterday.

The " Clipper Chip, " a microcircuit developed by the National
Institute of Standards and Technology, scrambles transmissions using a
mathematical formula. Two code numbers provide the keys that unlock
the scrambled transmissions. The two codes for a given chip will be
stored in two different government-run data bases, and court
permission will be required to get both.

In that way, the system meets objections of some who didn't want a
one- code system that would allow the government to listen in at will.
But it also avoids making calls so secret that police couldn't break
it.

Private companies will put the device into use. American Telephone &
Telegraph Co., which already leads the market in electronic
transmission encoders, plans to include the new chips in its $1,195
encryptors - small, boxy machines that attach to any phone. Several
other manufacturers are expected to sign up as well.

The chips will be manufactured by Mycotronx, a military contractor
based in Torrance, Calif.

Because of the current cost of complete encryption devices, the
initial market will be businesses and the government. Eventually,
cheaper units will become available for private use.

The demand for such machines has risen as cellular phones become more
and more popular and users realize that the phones are really just
radios, sending out signals that can be picked up by many scanners.
The cellular industry pushed for and got a federal law making it
illegal to pass on overheard conversations, but the availability of
technology and the American tradition of free access to radio signals
have rendered the law toothless.

