HEADLINE: Secret Phone Scheme Under Fire

BYLINE: Don Clark, Chronicle Staff Writer

KEYWORD: ENCRYPTION

BODY:

A Clinton administration proposal for a new lock and key on the
nation's communications networks set off a flood of questions and
complaints yesterday.

The administration proposed that government agencies and private
companies, such as banks and credit agencies, use a special microchip
that would be placed in telephones and other equipment to scramble
communications so they cannot be read by crooks, spies or hackers.

Unlike most previous encryption schemes, the system -- using a
microchip designed by a tiny Southern California company and
manufactured in San Jose -- uses mathematical formulas that were
developed by the super-secret National Security Agency and will remain
classified. And, for the first time, authorized law enforcement and
intelligence agencies would have the ability to crack such codes and
eavesdrop on criminals, terrorists and foreign governments.

Mathematical keys that unscramble coded data would be kept by two
independent government agencies. FBI agents conducting court approved
wiretaps could have access to the keys. The U.S. attorney general must
still designate the agencies, but observers said the Federal Reserve
Board and national laboratories might serve the purpose.

Civil libertarians and some high-tech companies immediately cried
foul, on grounds that classifying the formulas prevents independent
experts from testing the security of the new system. The NSA or other
agencies could theoretically keep a secret key -- known as a trap door
-- to be able to eavesdrop at will.

''A system based on classified, secret technology will not and should
not gain the confidence of the American public,'' said Mitchell Kapor,
former chairman of the Electronic Frontier Foundation, a Washington,
D.C., public policy group.

''Who is going to hold these keys?,'' asked Bruce Heiman, an attorney
representing the Software Publishers Association. ''How do we ensure
that they will be protected?''

''We applaud the administration's efforts to balance the needs of
privacy and law enforcement,'' said a spokesman for Representative Don
Edwards, a San Jose Democrat who chairs a House subcommittee that
oversees the FBI. ''There are some big questions that need to be
answered.''

RSA Data Security, a Redwood City- based company that sells encryption
software used by large computer and software companies, contends the
scheme is another in a series of NSA attempts to slow the spread of
software that the agency can't easily decode.

''This is an arrow aimed right at the heart of RSA,'' said James
Bidzos, its chief executive.

Not so, insisted Ray Kammer, acting director of the National Institute
of Standards and Technology, the Commerce Department agency
administering the encryption scheme. Kammer said that he came up with
the idea, and turned to NSA code experts to develop the technology.

Kammer said that the goal was to balance the needs of privacy and
public safety and find a successor to DES, the encryption formula used
for almost 20 years by government agencies and banks. By using 80 bits
of computer data -- rather than 56 bits -- the new formula would
theoretically take more than one billion years to crack using a
supercomputer, compared with 200 years using DES.

''There is no trap door,'' Kammer said.

NSA contracted with a little-known company in Torrance, Calif., to
make an encryption chip that could be used in telephones, fax machine
and computers. Mykotronx Inc., founded in 1979 by two former engineers
from TRW Inc., already sells classified encryption chips to protect
satellite communications.

San Jose-based VLSI Research Inc. will manufacture the chip, called
the Clipper. VLSI was chosen largely because it has a unique
manufacturing process that makes it nearly impossible to take the chip
apart and decode it.

American Telephone & Telegraph immediately announced it will use the
Clipper chip instead of DES in its products for scrambling telephone
calls.

The administration yesterday promised a broad review of all encryption
issues, including its own use of the Clipper technology and export
controls. Bidzos of RSA predicted that most high-tech companies will
keep using his company's software.

Companies, such as banks and credit agencies, may feel pressured to
adopt Clipper because of the government's endorsement, which some
observers view as premature. ''They've put the conclusion before the
process,'' said Daniel Weitsner, senior staff counsel for the
Electronic Frontier Foundation.

KEY QUESTIONS CONCERNING HIGH-TECH PRIVACY

Q. Why is information privacy important?

A. Spies, crooks and hackers can intercept phone conversations,
disrupt computer networks and steal information about businesses and
private citizens.

Q. How is privacy protected now?

A. Using a process called encryption, banks, businesses and government
organizations use special hardware or software to scramble
communications and computer data so it is unintelligible to others.

Q. Why change the current process?

A. Law enforcement officials and intelligence officials fear they
won't be able to intercept communications of criminals, terrorists and
foreign governments.

Q. What is the Clinton administration's proposal?

A. An encryption system based on a special chip and classified
mathematical formulas that is harder to break than existing systems.
Two mathematical keys that could crack the code would be kept by
separate agencies, and could be obtained by officials that have court
orders.

