HEADLINE: U.S. backs private use of phone code device

SOURCE: News Services

DATELINE: Washington, D.C.

BODY:

A telephone scrambling device, more sophisticated than anything on the
market, is being released by the government for private use. But this
one, unlike other encryptors, was built to enable wiretaps if
necessary.

The White House announced Friday that the " Clipper chip" developed by
the National Institute of Standards and Technology would provide
state-of-the-art protection against eavesdropping on corporate
secrets, but enable the FBI access if criminal activity is suspected.

Although computer encoding is used in only a small amount of
electronic communications, computer experts expect that volume to grow
rapidly as more of the nation's commerce begins to flow over data
networks - especially wireless networks that are particularly subject
to eavesdropping unless the information is coded.

AT&T, which makes more such security devices than anyone else, said it
would start putting the new technology in its products immediately.
"It makes sense for everyone to adopt this," said Thomas Brooks,
acting vice president of AT&T's information systems. Having a uniform
standard among the companies would make various brands of security
equipment compatible and be a sales advantage for any firm, he said.

One communications specialist was less sanguine about the development.

Stephen Bryen, a former Pentagon official and now president of Secured
Communications Technologies Inc. in Silver Spring, Md., which makes
data-security equipment, told the New York Times: "I think the
government is creating a monster. People won't be able to trust these
devices because there is a high risk that the government is going to
have complete access to anything they are going to do."

The chip is being manufactured by Mykotronx Inc., a military
contractor in Torrance, Calif., which was cleared for production by
the National Security Agency, the intelligence agency responsible for
spy satellites and communications intercepts.

No other firm has security clearance yet to make the chip, but other
vendors will be selected, said Raymond Kammer, acting director of the
institute of standards and technology.

Each " Clipper chip" device would have two unique numbers, or "keys"
that would be needed by authorized government agencies to decode the
transmissions. When manufacturers make the devices they would send the
two key numbers for each unit to a data base that would be established
by the attorney general.

The Clinton administration is assuring that only properly authorized
government officials would get access to the keys, and wiretapping
would be done only with legal authorization.

Kammer said a presidential review committee will monitor emergence of
the device onto the market and private reaction to it. "Our society
wants both secure communications and protection from organized crime,"
he said. Kammer said the government thinks this might be the answer.

